WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.420
What's the verdict on the new&nbsp;
"Night Court"? According to critics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:06.900
and fans of the original series,&nbsp;
watching it is a pretty hard time.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.500
No property is safe from the reboot&nbsp;
treatment. If something was even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.340
moderately popular back in the day, there's&nbsp;
a good chance a studio will try to bring it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.300
back to life in the 21st century. We've&nbsp;
seen this happen time and time again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.980
and 2023 kicked off with a reboot&nbsp;
few likely saw coming: "Night Court."

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:29.640
The original "Night Court" ran from 1984 to 1992,&nbsp;
and it saw a variety of cases go up in front of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.880
an inexperienced judge, often to humorous effect.&nbsp;
To kick off 2023, NBC has revived the property,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.440
but this time, Melissa Rauch of "The Big Bang&nbsp;
Theory" is playing the judge, the daughter of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.034
the original series' Judge Harold T. Stone,&nbsp;
who was played by the late Harry Anderson.

00:00:45.034 --> 00:00:47.700
"My name is Abby Stone. You&nbsp;
used to work with my dad."

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.260
The show also gets a leg up by bringing&nbsp;
back original cast member John Larroquette.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.160
The series premiered its first two&nbsp;
episodes on January 17, and it got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.840
pretty lukewarm reception from critics.&nbsp;
Amy Amatangelo of Paste Magazine wrote,

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.740
Fans of the original show weren't&nbsp;
especially happy either. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.820
unusual for new TV shows to require a&nbsp;
few episodes or even an entire season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.700
to find their footing. But even&nbsp;
with a certain amount of grace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.140
it's clear from social media that plenty of&nbsp;
"Night Court" fans just didn't like this new take.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.580
Someone on the show's subreddit asked their fellow&nbsp;
users what they thought about the first episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:31.680
and the consensus was that it was a vast&nbsp;
step down from the original. One replied,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.120
The series didn't fare much better&nbsp;
on Twitter, where plenty of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.140
checked out the first two episodes only&nbsp;
to walk away feeling dismayed. One wrote,

00:02:04.308 --> 00:02:07.128
"Break this down! Everybody out!"

00:02:07.128 --> 00:02:09.456
Another summed up their reaction in one word:

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.620
It's always possible that the&nbsp;
first two episodes simply stumbled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.220
and the season will get better. But a lot&nbsp;
of viewers may have already checked out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.360
and for those who stick around, the show will&nbsp;
face an uphill battle in trying to win them over.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.800
It doesn't help, according to some, that the&nbsp;
"Night Court" reboot doesn't adequately follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:30.900
up on the original series, despite clearly being&nbsp;
a continuation. There are plenty of callbacks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.400
its predecessor, including returning cast members&nbsp;
and cameos from Clarence, the stuffed armadillo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.600
that was ever-present throughout the original&nbsp;
series. And the original judge, Harry T. Stone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.980
is frequently mentioned by Rauch's Abby&nbsp;
Stone and Laroquette's Dan Fielding.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.920
But some fans need answers. One wrote on Twitter,

00:02:57.088 --> 00:03:00.820
"Bull, we decided to take you to Jupiter."

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:01.320
"Florida?"

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:05.340
"No, no, Bull. Jupiter, the planet."

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:09.660
The person who posted that tweet&nbsp;
was at least partially mistaken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:13.380
though. Bull was actually abducted in the&nbsp;
next-to-last episode of the series to air.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:17.700
There was one more episode in which he did&nbsp;
appear, with the abduction not mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.580
though the episode with the&nbsp;
abduction was the intended finale.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.800
Elsewhere, some fans are confused about Harry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.700
having a daughter who's old enough to be&nbsp;
a judge in the present day. One tweeted,

00:03:36.528 --> 00:03:39.960
They added, however, that "Night Court"&nbsp;
occurs in a universe with definitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:43.920
proof of God and extraterrestrial&nbsp;
life forms, so some things you just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.580
have to let go. Maybe Harry just had&nbsp;
a secret daughter for a few seasons.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.060
"This is bad. I wish this was an episode of 'Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.400
Court' 'cause then there'd&nbsp;
be some big joke right now."

